WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.206 --> 00:00:01.289
- He's an actor
(peaceful orchestral music)

00:00:01.289 --> 00:00:03.018
and producer best known for playing

00:00:03.018 --> 00:00:05.608
Jesse Pinkman in the Breaking Bad series.

00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:10.094
At 19, he drove to LA in
his 1982 Toyota Corolla

00:00:10.094 --> 00:00:13.070
with $6,000 in savings and his mother

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.395
to follow his Hollywood dream.

00:00:15.395 --> 00:00:18.072
He's the only actor to
ever win the Emmy Award

00:00:18.072 --> 00:00:21.253
for Outstanding Supporting
Actor in a Drama Series

00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:22.223
three times.

00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:26.125
He's Aaron Paul, and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:27.453
(whooshing)

00:00:27.453 --> 00:00:28.877
(echoing boom)

00:00:28.877 --> 00:00:29.710
(click)

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.750
- For longevity, if you want longevity,

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:32.583
(humming)

00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.273
you got to be picky with
the roles you choose.

00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:38.843
And it's all about it
has to be on the page.

00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:42.049
You know, if it's not written properly,

00:00:42.049 --> 00:00:43.830
then you have nothing.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.512
So if you get a script sent your way,

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:47.806
that they're interested in you,

00:00:47.806 --> 00:00:49.913
and it's really solid on the page,

00:00:49.913 --> 00:00:51.694
then you have something to work with.

00:00:51.694 --> 00:00:53.236
But other than that, then no.

00:00:53.236 --> 00:00:54.069
(click)

00:00:54.069 --> 00:00:57.321
I had so many ups and
downs, so many struggles,

00:00:57.321 --> 00:01:01.854
so much rejection, but to me,
I never saw myself going home.

00:01:01.854 --> 00:01:06.021
And to be honest, the first
time I ever asked my parents

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:09.565
for any sort of financial help,

00:01:09.565 --> 00:01:11.893
which was so hard for me to ask 'em,

00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:14.048
'cause this is my dream,
it's not their dream.

00:01:14.048 --> 00:01:16.280
And of course they want to see

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.082
their son succeed in their dreams.

00:01:18.082 --> 00:01:21.569
But first time I asked
them for any sort of help

00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:24.929
was right before booking Breaking Bad.

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:26.975
I was like "Could you
help me with my rent?"

00:01:26.975 --> 00:01:28.996
And they paid my rent
three months in a row,

00:01:28.996 --> 00:01:32.157
which was so hard for
me to allow them to do.

00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:34.761
And it was so hard for
them to do it, you know.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.349
They had nothing.

00:01:36.349 --> 00:01:37.825
And then I booked Breaking Bad,

00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:38.695
(energetic music)

00:01:38.695 --> 00:01:40.419
and you know, the rest is history.

00:01:40.419 --> 00:01:41.252
(click)

00:01:41.252 --> 00:01:42.327
When I first moved here, they're like

00:01:42.327 --> 00:01:44.817
"Listen, all you got to
know is the strong survive,

00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:48.131
"and if you're strong
enough, you'll survive."

00:01:48.131 --> 00:01:49.835
And so I have always remembered that.

00:01:49.835 --> 00:01:50.668
(click)

00:01:50.668 --> 00:01:52.024
There's been times where I was like wow,

00:01:52.024 --> 00:01:54.467
this is really hard, this is really hard.

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:57.638
I mean, just the ups and
downs, and where I'm like wow.

00:01:57.638 --> 00:01:59.651
I'll get like back-to-back jobs,

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:02.416
I'm like my career is
like really taking off!

00:02:02.416 --> 00:02:05.302
And then there'll just be a standstill,

00:02:05.302 --> 00:02:08.944
and it's just like roll with the punches

00:02:08.944 --> 00:02:11.033
and just deal with the rejection.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.236
And it took a minute to understand

00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:14.979
that that's such a huge
part of this business.

00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.636
And now I just don't take it personally.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.794
I just like you know what,

00:02:17.794 --> 00:02:19.461
there was someone better for the role,

00:02:19.461 --> 00:02:21.474
and there's another job out there.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.307
(click)

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:25.320
There was doing church plays
since I was a little itty baby.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.217
(murmuring)
I was the baby Jesus.

00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:30.807
I don't know if I knew then, but I think

00:02:30.807 --> 00:02:33.881
around like eight or
nine, when I was doing

00:02:33.881 --> 00:02:37.277
kind of like the leading
roles, I was obsessed with it

00:02:37.277 --> 00:02:38.951
and kind of just ran with it.

00:02:38.951 --> 00:02:39.784
(click)

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:42.087
When I get the script,
I just kind of dissect.

00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:45.804
I read it just front to
back, and then I start

00:02:45.804 --> 00:02:49.884
really dissecting each scene
and kind of understanding

00:02:49.884 --> 00:02:52.336
why he's in this particular situation,

00:02:52.336 --> 00:02:53.610
why he's feeling, these sort of things.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.459
But it's all so much on the page.

00:02:55.459 --> 00:02:59.146
And so I just, how I dive
into a particular scene,

00:02:59.146 --> 00:03:03.045
I just try and live those
moments as if I were Jesse.

00:03:03.045 --> 00:03:05.067
I don't try and have certain triggers

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:07.992
or think about some sort of torture

00:03:07.992 --> 00:03:10.893
that I personally have
went through in my past.

00:03:10.893 --> 00:03:13.901
I just try and really live and breathe

00:03:13.901 --> 00:03:15.893
through this particular person's skin.

00:03:15.893 --> 00:03:19.275
And with Jesse, it's a constant struggle.

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:22.284
And so it's a little rough at times,

00:03:22.284 --> 00:03:24.025
but it's such a blast.

00:03:24.025 --> 00:03:24.858
(click)

00:03:24.858 --> 00:03:28.132
I always tend to gravitate towards

00:03:28.132 --> 00:03:31.196
the more complex side of things,

00:03:31.196 --> 00:03:33.529
just complicated characters,

00:03:34.508 --> 00:03:38.675
just because for me, it's
just more interesting to play.

00:03:39.638 --> 00:03:40.471
(click)

00:03:40.471 --> 00:03:41.502
A lot of roles are coming my way

00:03:41.502 --> 00:03:44.797
where I'm playing like
the lovable drug addict.

00:03:44.797 --> 00:03:48.015
But what I want to do is do
something completely opposite,

00:03:48.015 --> 00:03:50.333
just do something that is different

00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:52.325
than something I've ever done,

00:03:52.325 --> 00:03:55.544
something that's kind of
terrifying and scary to me.

00:03:55.544 --> 00:03:57.698
But that's what it's about.

00:03:57.698 --> 00:04:01.852
Gravitate towards just such,
(murmuring)

00:04:01.852 --> 00:04:04.465
just different kind of roles, ya know.

00:04:04.465 --> 00:04:09.102
Like I try to do stuff that's
always kind of terrifying.

00:04:09.102 --> 00:04:11.129
Not terrifying as a story,

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:14.405
but it just terrifies
me as an actor to play.

00:04:14.405 --> 00:04:15.238
(click)

00:04:15.238 --> 00:04:18.563
Embrace every moment, 'cause it flies by.

00:04:18.563 --> 00:04:19.736
Just embrace everything.
(humming)

00:04:19.736 --> 00:04:22.035
And I've really taken that to heart,

00:04:22.035 --> 00:04:24.315
and I have embraced everything.

00:04:24.315 --> 00:04:25.148
(click)

00:04:25.148 --> 00:04:28.289
- Most of us have recovered
enough to go on, Rod.

00:04:28.289 --> 00:04:29.813
(laughing) Who's our next player?!

00:04:29.813 --> 00:04:32.187
- [Rod] Well, let's see what
we can do for Aaron Sturtevant!

00:04:32.187 --> 00:04:33.020
(joyful jazzy music)
(cheering)

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.548
(applause)
Come on down!

00:04:34.548 --> 00:04:38.715
Aaron, you're the next
contestant on The Price Is Right!

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:46.274
(laughing)

00:04:46.274 --> 00:04:48.092
- Oh! (grunting)

00:04:48.092 --> 00:04:49.684
You're the man, Bob!

00:04:49.684 --> 00:04:51.392
Oh my, you're my idol!

00:04:51.392 --> 00:04:54.496
- [Bob] I have the horrible
feeling I have another one!

00:04:54.496 --> 00:04:55.365
(laughing)
(applause)

00:04:55.365 --> 00:04:57.500
(cheering)

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.083
Here's the next item up for bid.

00:04:59.083 --> 00:05:00.254
(jazzy music)

00:05:00.254 --> 00:05:02.958
- [Rod] An elegant flat top desk!

00:05:02.958 --> 00:05:05.545
(cheering)
(applause)

00:05:05.545 --> 00:05:07.982
- (laughing) Aaron, what
do you bid on that?!

00:05:07.982 --> 00:05:09.284
- 1200 bucks! (yelling)

00:05:09.284 --> 00:05:10.640
- 1200 bucks!
(ding)

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.780
(applause)
Michelle.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.409
- 775, Bob.

00:05:14.409 --> 00:05:15.794
- [Bob] 775!
(ding)

00:05:15.794 --> 00:05:16.627
Charleta.

00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:17.460
- 800.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.474
- [Bob] $800.
(ding)

00:05:18.474 --> 00:05:20.057
Tracy!

00:05:20.057 --> 00:05:20.890
- 850!

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.889
- Her bid is 850.
(ding)

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:26.413
Actual retail price, 1,797!
(cheering)

00:05:26.413 --> 00:05:27.776
(joyful jazzy music)
Aaron wins!

00:05:27.776 --> 00:05:29.557
(dinging)
(applause)

00:05:29.557 --> 00:05:31.033
Aaron is the winner.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:33.033
Let's get him over here!

00:05:33.896 --> 00:05:35.380
Right here, Aaron!

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.672
Congratulations!

00:05:36.672 --> 00:05:38.632
- I'm touching Bob Barker!

00:05:38.632 --> 00:05:39.465
(laughing)
Yeah!

00:05:39.465 --> 00:05:40.747
(yelling)
- Aaron!

00:05:40.747 --> 00:05:41.580
- Yes?

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.082
- Aaron, believe it or not,
I have another one of these!

00:05:45.082 --> 00:05:46.404
- [Rod] A new car!
(dramatic joyful jazzy music)

00:05:46.404 --> 00:05:50.571
(cheering)
(applause)

00:05:54.173 --> 00:05:56.519
- Alright, we're going
to play the Dice Game.

00:05:56.519 --> 00:05:59.151
Light up the first number in
the price of the car, please!

00:05:59.151 --> 00:05:59.984
- [Woman] Come on Aaron!

00:05:59.984 --> 00:06:00.817
(ding)

00:06:00.817 --> 00:06:01.808
- Here are the dice.

00:06:01.808 --> 00:06:05.102
Now, remember there are no
zeroes in the price of the car.

00:06:05.102 --> 00:06:05.935
- Okay!

00:06:05.935 --> 00:06:07.836
- There are no numbers higher than six.

00:06:07.836 --> 00:06:08.669
- Yes!

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:11.342
- Role, and let's hope it's
the second number in the price.

00:06:11.342 --> 00:06:12.198
- [Woman] Go Aaron!

00:06:12.198 --> 00:06:13.298
- The first number is one.
(cheering)

00:06:13.298 --> 00:06:14.433
Now you've rolled a one.
(buzzing)

00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:15.854
Is that the second number?
(applause)

00:06:15.854 --> 00:06:17.312
(yelling)
No.

00:06:17.312 --> 00:06:20.220
Is the second number
higher or lower than one?

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:21.053
- Higher, higher!

00:06:21.053 --> 00:06:22.476
- Of course, it has to be.
(ding)

00:06:22.476 --> 00:06:25.337
Okay, next number in the price of the car.

00:06:25.337 --> 00:06:26.343
Is it six?
(cheering)

00:06:26.343 --> 00:06:27.203
(applause)
(buzzing)

00:06:27.203 --> 00:06:28.036
No!

00:06:28.036 --> 00:06:29.464
Is it higher?
(yelling)

00:06:29.464 --> 00:06:31.318
Lower than six!

00:06:31.318 --> 00:06:32.151
Roll!

00:06:32.151 --> 00:06:33.666
Aaron, we're on a hot streak!
(ding)

00:06:33.666 --> 00:06:35.249
Is it three?
(groaning)

00:06:35.249 --> 00:06:36.429
(buzzing)
No.

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:38.527
Is it higher or lower than three?

00:06:38.527 --> 00:06:39.360
(yelling)

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.193
- Higher!

00:06:40.193 --> 00:06:41.343
- Higher than three.

00:06:41.343 --> 00:06:42.176
(ding)

00:06:42.176 --> 00:06:43.712
Alright, roll that last number.

00:06:43.712 --> 00:06:45.264
Is it four?!
(groaning)

00:06:45.264 --> 00:06:46.145
(buzzing)

00:06:46.145 --> 00:06:46.978
No!

00:06:46.978 --> 00:06:48.511
Is it higher or lower than four?

00:06:48.511 --> 00:06:49.344
(yelling)
- Lower!

00:06:49.344 --> 00:06:50.302
- Lower than four.

00:06:50.302 --> 00:06:51.602
Let's find out.
(ding)

00:06:51.602 --> 00:06:52.627
Does Aaron win the car?

00:06:52.627 --> 00:06:54.727
Light up the second number, please.

00:06:54.727 --> 00:06:55.560
(ding)
(cheering)

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.021
Light up the third number, please.

00:06:57.021 --> 00:06:57.854
(applause)
- [Woman] Come on Aaron!

00:06:57.854 --> 00:06:58.687
(ding)

00:06:58.687 --> 00:07:00.540
- Give us a number higher than three!

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:01.507
(ding)
(cheering)

00:07:01.507 --> 00:07:02.340
Yes!
(applause)

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.931
Give us a number lower than four!

00:07:04.931 --> 00:07:06.112
(buzzing)
Oh!

00:07:06.112 --> 00:07:07.650
(low horn melody)
(groaning)

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.067
Aaron, I'm sorry!

00:07:11.031 --> 00:07:12.420
- Oh!
(joyful jazzy music)

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.789
(laughing)

00:07:13.789 --> 00:07:15.092
I get to spin the wheel, right?!

00:07:15.092 --> 00:07:16.165
- That's right!
(cheering)

00:07:16.165 --> 00:07:17.353
He'll spin the wheel!
(cheering)

00:07:17.353 --> 00:07:19.029
(applause)
We'll be back after this!

00:07:19.029 --> 00:07:20.264
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:07:20.264 --> 00:07:21.289
I made this video
(peaceful orchestral music)

00:07:21.289 --> 00:07:24.086
because Orion Dark Night asked me too.

00:07:24.086 --> 00:07:25.551
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:07:25.551 --> 00:07:27.035
that you want me to profile next,

00:07:27.035 --> 00:07:28.226
leave it in the comments below

00:07:28.226 --> 00:07:29.701
and I'll see what I can do.

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:32.211
I'd also love to know
which of Aaron Paul's

00:07:32.211 --> 00:07:35.151
top 10 rules resonated the most with you.

00:07:35.151 --> 00:07:36.157
Leave it below,

00:07:36.157 --> 00:07:37.555
I'm going to join the discussion.

00:07:37.555 --> 00:07:39.260
Thank you for watching, I'll see you soon.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:40.404
(whooshing)

00:07:40.404 --> 00:07:42.235
(echoing boom)

